			"properties:10": {
				"betterquesting:10": {
					"notification_pos_y:3": -2147483648,
					"notification_subtitle_scale:5": -1.0,
					"visibility:8": "NORMAL",
					"partySingleReward:1": 0,
					"notification_style:8": "default",
					"completion_particle:8": "default",
					"notification_duration:5": -1.0,
					"isMain:1": 0,
					"lockedProgress:1": 0,
					"repeat_relative:1": 1,
					"notification_fade_out:5": -1.0,
					"snd_complete:8": "random.levelup",
					"repeatTime:3": -1,
					"notification_pos_x:3": -2147483648,
					"particle_count:3": -1,
					"countAsQuest:1": 1,
					"autoClaim:1": 0,
					"globalShare:1": 1,
					"desc:8": "Heat vents are a passive way to remove heat from your reactor. Their performance is measured in heat units per tick (Hu/t), and there are a few different types that all work slightly differently.\n\nThese heat vents simply cool themselves after absorbing heat from adjacent fuel rods and heat exchangers:\n\nHeat Vent: Cools itself by 6 Hu/t\n\nAdvanced Heat Vent: Cools itself by 12 Hu/t\n\nThese heat vents absorb heat and cool themselves much like the ones above, but they also pull heat directly from the reactor core to themselves:\n\nReactor Heat Vent: Cools itself by 5 Hu/t and the reactor by 5 Hu/t\n\nOverclocked Heat Vent: Cools itself by 20 Hu/t and the reactor by 36 Hu/t[note]Since it pulls more heat from the reactor than it dissipates, these are prone to melting if their heat is not balanced by a heat exchanger[/note]\n\nThe Component Heat Vent works differently to the other vents. Rather than absorbing heat and cooling itself or the reactor, it simply cools adjacent components (but not fuel rods) by 4 Hu/t",
					"notification_icon_scale:5": -1.0,
					"notification_show_icon:8": "default",
					"notification_fade_in:5": -1.0,
					"icon:10": {
						"id:8": "IC2:reactorVentDiamond",
						"Count:3": 1,
						"Damage:2": 1,
						"OreDict:8": ""
					},
